window.COMMERCE_REVIEW_LESSONS=[{"id":"commerce-occupations","old":{"title":"Commerce, trade and occupations","body":"Commerce consists of trade and the activities that assist trade. Trade is buying and selling goods and services. The aids to trade include banking, insurance, transport, warehousing, communication and advertising. Trade is the centre of commerce: the aids make exchange easier, safer and more regular. Commerce connects production with consumption; it does not mean manufacturing alone.\n\nAn occupation is a lawful activity through which a person earns a living. Industrial occupations obtain raw materials, change their form or construct things. Farming, mining, manufacturing and building are examples. Commercial occupations distribute goods and provide aids to trade. Retailers, wholesalers, bankers and transport operators perform commercial work. Service occupations provide personal or professional assistance. A teacher, doctor or lawyer gives a direct service to the person who needs it.\n\nDirect services satisfy wants directly. Commercial services assist the process of exchange. A lawyer advising a client is usually classified as giving a direct professional service in school Commerce. Do not classify every paid activity as trade merely because money changes hands. The question may be asking about the nature of the work, not its payment.\n\nCommerce developed as people produced surpluses and exchanged them. Barter is the direct exchange of goods for goods. It is limited by the need for a double coincidence of wants: each trader must want what the other offers. Money removes much of that difficulty. Better roads, communication, security, banking and storage support larger markets and greater specialization.\n\nWorked example: a farmer grows maize, a mill turns it into flour, and a shop sells the flour. Growing is primary production, milling is secondary production, and the shop performs trade. The bank financing the shop supplies an aid to trade. Together these activities help consumers obtain a useful product.\n\nPublic services such as policing and law-making benefit society collectively and are traditionally called indirect services. Private professional assistance to an individual is a direct service.\n\nAdvertising expands awareness and demand, supporting trade; sustainable growth also depends on production, distribution, finance and wider conditions.\n\nCommerce can generate government revenue through lawful taxes and charges, helping finance public services and development.\n\nTrade creates interdependence between nations and can encourage cooperation and friendly relationships. It does not guarantee that every international disagreement disappears.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Commerce is wider than trade but is not the same as all production.\n• Trade is the buying-and-selling part; manufacturing changes the form of goods.\n• Commercial activity includes services, not only physical goods.\n• The reward earned does not determine the occupational category. Examine what the worker actually does."},"new":{"title":"Commerce, trade and occupations","body":"COMMERCE, TRADE AND OCCUPATIONS — HOW GOODS REACH THE CONSUMER\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nA farmer can produce a thousand bags of rice, yet families in another state may still be unable to buy them. Production alone does not guarantee that goods reach the people who need them. Commerce solves the problems of exchange, movement, storage, payment, information and risk. In JAMB, the key is to identify precisely what each business activity does.\n\n1. MEANING AND SCOPE OF COMMERCE\nCommerce is the branch of business activity concerned with the exchange and distribution of goods and services, together with the activities that facilitate exchange. Its two major divisions are TRADE and AIDS TO TRADE.\n\nTrade is buying and selling goods and services. A trader normally acquires goods for resale or connects buyers and sellers. A shopkeeper selling exercise books performs trade; the printer manufacturing them performs production.\n\nAids to trade are services that make trade possible, convenient or less risky:\n• Transport moves goods between places and creates place utility.\n• Warehousing stores goods until they are needed and creates time utility.\n• Banking provides payment services, savings facilities and credit.\n• Insurance provides financial protection against specified risks, subject to policy terms.\n• Advertising informs or persuades potential buyers.\n• Communication transfers orders, quotations, complaints and other commercial information.\n\nEXAMPLE — FROM KANO TO PORT HARCOURT\nA tomato farmer harvests tomatoes in Kano. A merchant buys them for resale (trade). A refrigerated vehicle carries them to Port Harcourt (transport). A bank transfers the purchase price (banking). A phone call confirms delivery arrangements (communication). An insurer may cover specified transit losses (insurance). A shop displays a price promotion (advertising). Each activity has a different function, even though they support the same transaction.\n\n2. COMMERCE, INDUSTRY AND BUSINESS: THE DIFFERENCE\nBusiness is the broad activity of producing or supplying goods and services, usually to earn income or meet an organisational objective. Industry is primarily concerned with extracting, processing, manufacturing or constructing goods. Commerce is concerned with exchange and distribution.\n\nA bakery making bread is engaged in secondary production. The same bakery selling bread directly to customers also undertakes trade. A business can therefore perform more than one function.\n\nProduction creates or increases usefulness (utility):\n• Form utility: changing raw materials into a more useful product, as when timber becomes furniture.\n• Place utility: making a product available where consumers want it, through transport.\n• Time utility: making a product available when needed, through storage.\n• Possession utility: transferring ownership or the right to use a product through exchange.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Do not say that commerce manufactures goods. Manufacturing is industrial production; commerce helps bring goods and services to the market.\n\n3. TRADE: HOME AND FOREIGN\nHome (domestic or internal) trade takes place within a country. It includes wholesale trade, which commonly involves purchasing in bulk and selling to retailers or other business users, and retail trade, which involves selling in smaller quantities to final consumers.\n\nForeign (international or external) trade crosses national borders:\n• Import trade: buying goods or services from abroad.\n• Export trade: selling goods or services to buyers abroad.\n• Entrepôt trade: importing goods and subsequently re-exporting them, usually with little or no substantial processing.\n\nEXAMPLE: A Nigerian dealer buying shoes from another Nigerian dealer conducts home trade. Buying shoes from Italy is import trade from Nigeria's perspective. Selling Nigerian cocoa to a buyer in the Netherlands is export trade.\n\nA wholesaler does not always sell only to retailers, and a retailer does not always operate from a physical shop. Classify the transaction by the function performed.\n\n4. OCCUPATION: MEANING AND MAJOR CLASSES\nAn occupation is a regular activity through which a person earns a livelihood. For school Commerce, occupations are commonly grouped into industrial, commercial and service occupations.\n\nINDUSTRIAL OCCUPATIONS\nThese involve obtaining materials from nature, transforming them or constructing structures.\n• Extractive or primary: farming, fishing, mining, forestry.\n• Manufacturing or processing: milling rice, making soap, producing cement.\n• Construction: building bridges, houses and roads.\n\nCOMMERCIAL OCCUPATIONS\nThese involve buying and selling or providing services that assist exchange: wholesalers, retailers, bankers, insurance workers, transporters, warehouse operators and advertising professionals.\n\nSERVICE OCCUPATIONS\nThese supply services rather than primarily producing or distributing physical goods. Direct or personal services are provided to identifiable clients, such as medical treatment, teaching, hairdressing or legal advice. Indirect or public services serve the wider community, such as public security and certain government administrative functions.\n\nIMPORTANT DISTINCTION: Banking and transport are services in the broad economic sense, but school Commerce often groups them specifically under commercial occupations because they are aids to trade. A doctor treating a patient is ordinarily classified as providing a direct service, not as a trader merely because the patient pays.\n\n5. DIVISION OF LABOUR AND INTERDEPENDENCE\nSpecialisation means concentrating on a particular task or economic activity. As workers and firms specialise, they depend on others for goods and services they do not produce themselves.\n\nConsider a school uniform: a cotton grower provides raw material, a textile factory makes cloth, a tailor sews the uniform, a distributor transports it, and a shop sells it. No single occupation necessarily performs every step.\n\nSpecialisation can increase productivity and skill, but it also creates interdependence. If transport stops, a retailer may run out of stock even when the factory is still producing. Commerce connects specialised producers to consumers.\n\n6. HOW COMMERCE DEVELOPED\nIn subsistence economies, families produced much of what they consumed. As specialisation and surplus production increased, people exchanged goods.\n\nBARTER is the direct exchange of goods or services for other goods or services without money. Its difficulties include:\n• Double coincidence of wants: each party must want what the other offers.\n• Lack of a common measure of value: comparing unlike goods is difficult.\n• Indivisibility: a cow cannot always be divided conveniently to pay for a small item.\n• Difficulty storing some goods as wealth: perishable goods spoil.\n• Difficulty arranging deferred payments: agreeing what to repay later can be problematic.\n\nMoney developed as a generally accepted medium of exchange. Its principal functions are medium of exchange, unit of account, store of value and standard of deferred payment. Money makes exchange easier but does not eliminate scarcity or guarantee stable purchasing power.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO\nAda has a bag of beans and wants shoes. Tunde sells shoes but wants a bicycle, not beans. They cannot barter directly because their wants do not coincide. If Ada sells the beans for money, she can use the proceeds to buy shoes without needing the shoe seller to want beans.\n\n7. IMPORTANCE OF COMMERCE TO AN ECONOMY\nCommerce:\n• Connects producers with customers and widens markets.\n• Makes specialised production more practical.\n• Creates jobs in distribution, banking, insurance and related services.\n• Reduces some exchange risks and transaction difficulties.\n• Makes goods available at appropriate places and times.\n• Encourages investment and can increase business income.\n• Facilitates domestic and international trade.\n• Generates tax and other lawful government revenue.\n• May strengthen economic links between regions and countries.\n\nThese are potential contributions, not guarantees. Poor roads, insecurity, high transaction costs or weak institutions can prevent commerce from functioning effectively.\n\n8. COMPARISON GUIDE\nTrade versus commerce: trade is buying and selling; commerce includes trade plus aids to trade.\nIndustry versus commerce: industry obtains or transforms goods; commerce facilitates their exchange and distribution.\nWholesaler versus retailer: wholesalers generally sell in bulk to resellers or organisations; retailers generally sell to final consumers.\nDirect versus indirect services: direct services benefit identifiable clients; indirect/public services benefit the community collectively.\nBarter versus monetary exchange: barter swaps items directly; monetary exchange uses money as the intermediary.\n\n9. JAMB-STYLE APPLICATIONS\nQ1. A firm keeps bags of rice after harvest so they can be sold during the dry season. Which aid to trade is involved?\nAnswer: Warehousing. The main benefit is time utility.\n\nQ2. A furniture factory changes timber into tables. Is this commerce?\nAnswer: The transformation is secondary production. Selling the finished tables is trade.\n\nQ3. A lawyer advises a customer about a tenancy agreement. Which occupation is illustrated?\nAnswer: Direct professional service, even though the lawyer receives payment.\n\nQ4. A Nigerian company buys goods abroad and sells them to a third country without substantial processing. What is the trade called?\nAnswer: Entrepôt or re-export trade.\n\nQ5. Why does barter require a double coincidence of wants?\nAnswer: Each participant must simultaneously desire the item the other participant offers.\n\nQ6. What is the difference between place and time utility?\nAnswer: Transport creates place utility by changing location; warehousing creates time utility by changing when goods are available.\n\n10. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Commerce is broader than trade, but it is not synonymous with every kind of business activity.\n• Manufacturing is not an aid to trade; transport and banking are.\n• A paid occupation is not automatically trade.\n• A service can belong to a commercial occupation when its role is to assist trade.\n• Foreign trade is classified from the viewpoint of the country concerned: one country's export is another country's import.\n• Entrepôt trade involves re-export, not ordinary local retailing.\n• Warehousing does not create form utility; manufacturing commonly does.\n• Money reduces barter difficulties but does not remove the need for resources.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nCOMMERCE = TRADE + AIDS TO TRADE.\nAsk three questions about any activity: Is something being MADE, EXCHANGED, or HELPING EXCHANGE? That usually identifies industry, trade, or an aid to trade."}},{"id":"production-factors","old":{"title":"Production, factors and specialization","body":"Production is the creation of goods and services that satisfy human wants. It also creates or adds utility, meaning usefulness. A product is not fully useful to its consumer merely because it has left a factory. Transport creates place utility; warehousing creates time utility by keeping goods until they are needed.\n\nPrimary production obtains products from nature. Farming, fishing, forestry and mining are examples. Secondary production changes raw materials into more useful forms or constructs fixed structures. Manufacturing turns cotton into cloth; construction provides roads and buildings. Manufacturing can assemble factory components; construction assembles manufactured materials into usable works. A stem saying only “assembling products” needs context to distinguish these categories. Tertiary production provides services. Trade, banking, transport and direct professional services fall within the service sector.\n\nThe factors of production are land, labour, capital and enterprise. Land includes natural resources, not just soil. Its supply is relatively fixed from the economy's viewpoint, although particular land can be improved or reclaimed. Its reward is rent. Labour is human physical and mental effort used in production; its reward is wages or salaries. Workers can move between places or occupations, although training and family ties may restrict mobility.\n\nCapital means human-made resources used for further production. Machines, tools and factory buildings are capital goods. Money finances their purchase, but money is not itself a productive machine. The reward for using borrowed capital is interest. The entrepreneur organizes the other factors, makes business decisions and bears business risk. Profit is the reward for successful enterprise, while losses are possible.\n\nDivision of labour breaks production into separate tasks performed by different workers. Specialization means concentrating on a particular activity, product or area of work. Division of labour is one form of specialization. It can increase skill, save time and permit the use of machinery. However, repetitive work can cause boredom, and a failure at one stage can delay the whole process.\n\nWorked example: one worker cuts material, another sews and another checks finished school uniforms. This is division of labour. The machines are capital, the workers supply labour, and the owner who organizes production acts as entrepreneur.\n\nCapital goods are human-made production aids; intermediate goods are inputs used up or transformed in making other goods. In the economic long run, all factors can vary. Aptitude means natural ability for a particular occupation.\n\nFabrication forms or assembles materials into products; conversion changes their form. Use the particular transformation described when both terms appear among options.\n\nSpecialization can reduce craftsmanship.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Secondary production includes construction as well as manufacturing.\n• Capital goods assist further production; consumer goods satisfy wants directly.\n• The entrepreneur's main role is coordination and risk-bearing, not supplying every factor personally."},"new":{"title":"Production, factors and specialization","body":"PRODUCTION, FACTORS AND SPECIALISATION — HOW BUSINESSES CREATE VALUE\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nBefore goods can be bought and sold, somebody must make them available. Production, factors of production and division of labour explain how that happens. JAMB often presents a practical situation—a quarry, a tailoring workshop or a transport firm—and asks students to classify its activities, identify the relevant factor or explain the benefit or limitation of specialisation.\n\n1. MEANING OF PRODUCTION\nProduction is the creation or addition of utility through goods and services that satisfy human wants. Utility means usefulness or want-satisfying power. Production is not restricted to factory work: farming produces crops, a teacher provides education, and a transporter makes goods available in another place.\n\nFour useful forms of utility:\n• Form utility: processing or transforming materials, such as turning cassava into garri.\n• Place utility: moving goods to where they are wanted, such as transporting tomatoes from Jos to Lagos.\n• Time utility: storing goods until they are needed, such as keeping grain after harvest.\n• Possession utility: making ownership or the right to use goods available through exchange, such as selling a bicycle to a customer.\n\nBe careful: these describe different ways usefulness is added. A retailer may contribute possession utility without changing a product's physical form.\n\n2. STAGES OF PRODUCTION\nPRIMARY PRODUCTION obtains resources from nature. Examples include crop farming, fishing, forestry, mining and quarrying. Its output often supplies raw materials to other industries.\n\nSECONDARY PRODUCTION transforms raw materials or creates structures.\n• Manufacturing: cotton into cloth; cocoa beans into chocolate; metal into cooking pots.\n• Construction: building roads, bridges, houses and dams.\nManufacturing may include assembly. Construction is distinguished by producing structures or works, often fixed at their locations.\n\nTERTIARY PRODUCTION supplies services. It includes commercial services such as banking, transport, insurance, retailing and communication, as well as direct services such as medical treatment and education.\n\nPRODUCTION CHAIN — FOLLOW A BAG OF RICE\nFARMER (primary: grows paddy)\n  ↓\nRICE MILL (secondary: removes husk and processes rice)\n  ↓\nTRANSPORTER (tertiary: moves bags to market)\n  ↓\nWHOLESALER → RETAILER (tertiary trade)\n  ↓\nHOUSEHOLD (final consumer)\n\nThis is a simplified flow diagram, not a rule that every product must pass through every intermediary. A farmer can sell directly to consumers.\n\nJAMB APPLICATION: A firm that mines limestone performs primary production. A cement factory processing limestone performs secondary production. A lorry carrying cement to a dealer provides tertiary services.\n\n3. FACTORS OF PRODUCTION: THE FOUR INPUTS\nFactors of production are resources used to produce goods and services. The conventional school-Commerce classification is LAND, LABOUR, CAPITAL and ENTERPRISE.\n\nLAND\nLand means all natural resources used in production, not merely the ground beneath a building. It includes farmland, rivers, minerals, forests and natural fishing grounds.\nCharacteristics:\n• It is a gift of nature rather than a human-made capital good.\n• Its total natural supply is relatively fixed, though usable land can be improved, reclaimed or reallocated.\n• Its quality and location differ.\n• It is geographically immobile, although its use may change.\nReward: RENT, particularly payment for the use of land or natural resources.\n\nLABOUR\nLabour is human physical or mental effort used in producing goods and services. A mason, accountant, teacher and machine operator all supply labour.\nCharacteristics:\n• Labour is inseparable from the worker.\n• Skill, training, health and experience affect productivity.\n• Labour may move between occupations or places, but mobility is restricted by qualifications, language, family circumstances and other barriers.\n• Labour is perishable in the sense that an unused hour of labour cannot be stored for later.\nReward: WAGES or SALARIES.\n\nCAPITAL\nCapital consists of human-made resources used to produce other goods and services: machines, tools, production buildings, vehicles and equipment.\n• Fixed capital: durable productive assets used repeatedly, such as factory machinery.\n• Working or circulating capital: resources used up or regularly replaced in the production cycle, such as raw materials and supplies. In business finance, working capital also commonly means current assets minus current liabilities; do not confuse the two contexts.\n• Physical capital: productive equipment and structures.\n• Financial capital: funds available to acquire productive assets and finance operations.\n\nReward: INTEREST is conventionally associated with capital in factor-reward questions, especially lending. An owner may also earn other returns depending on how capital is employed.\n\nMoney is not itself a machine. It enables a business to buy machines and materials. Likewise, a bag of cement used to build a house is an intermediate input, while a cement mixer repeatedly used on projects is a capital good.\n\nENTERPRISE OR ENTREPRENEURSHIP\nEnterprise is the function of organising land, labour and capital, making decisions, innovating and bearing business risk.\nAn entrepreneur may:\n• Identify a market opportunity.\n• Combine resources and arrange finance.\n• Choose products, prices and production methods.\n• Hire workers and supervise operations.\n• Bear uncertainty about sales, costs and competition.\nReward: PROFIT when the venture succeeds; LOSS is possible when it fails.\n\nFACTORS AND REWARDS — MEMORY TABLE\nLand → Rent\nLabour → Wages or salaries\nCapital → Interest\nEnterprise → Profit (or possible loss)\n\nThese are conventional examination associations. In actual businesses, an individual may supply several factors and receive different types of income.\n\n4. FIXED CAPITAL, CONSUMER GOODS AND INTERMEDIATE GOODS\nA good's classification depends on HOW IT IS USED.\n• Consumer good: bought for direct satisfaction of wants, such as a family's refrigerator.\n• Capital good: used to produce or distribute goods and services, such as a refrigerator in a restaurant kitchen.\n• Intermediate good: used up or transformed in making another product, such as flour used by a bakery.\n\nA vehicle used for commercial deliveries is a capital good for the transport business; a family car used privately is primarily a consumer durable. The same physical object can therefore have different economic classifications.\n\n5. SPECIALISATION AND DIVISION OF LABOUR\nSpecialisation means concentrating on a particular job, skill, product or branch of production. It can occur among individuals, firms, regions and countries.\n\nDivision of labour means splitting a production process into distinct tasks assigned to different workers or groups. It is a form of specialisation, but not all specialisation is division of labour. A country specialising in cocoa exports does not necessarily divide a single factory process among workers.\n\nTAILORING WORKSHOP — TASK FLOW DIAGRAM\nMEASURE CUSTOMER\n  ↓\nCUT FABRIC\n  ↓\nSEW GARMENT\n  ↓\nATTACH BUTTONS / ZIP\n  ↓\nPRESS AND INSPECT\n  ↓\nDELIVER TO CUSTOMER\n\nIf different workers specialise in these stages, the workshop practises division of labour. If one tailor completes every stage, the process is not divided among workers, even if that tailor specialises in making uniforms.\n\n6. ADVANTAGES OF DIVISION OF LABOUR\n• Repetition can improve skill and speed.\n• Workers save time otherwise spent switching between tasks.\n• Specialised machines may be used efficiently.\n• Training for a narrow task may be faster.\n• Large-scale production can become easier.\n• Output per worker may rise, lowering unit cost.\n• Standardisation and quality checks can improve consistency.\n\nThese gains depend on good coordination, reliable materials and sufficient demand. Division of labour does not automatically increase quality or wages.\n\n7. DISADVANTAGES AND LIMITATIONS\n• Repetitive tasks may cause boredom and reduce motivation.\n• Workers may lose the ability to make an entire product.\n• A stoppage in one stage can interrupt the whole production line.\n• Highly specialised workers may struggle to change jobs.\n• Excessive dependence on machines or other workers can create vulnerability.\n• Work may become monotonous or reduce individual craftsmanship.\n\nSpecialisation is more effective when the market is large enough to support sustained demand, capital and equipment are available, workers can be trained, and transport and communication are reliable.\n\nEXAMPLE: A bakery with 30 workers can assign teams to mixing, baking, packaging and sales. A village bakery with only one worker and very few customers may find such a division impractical.\n\n8. SCALE OF PRODUCTION\nSmall-scale production generally uses fewer resources and serves a limited market. It can be flexible and close to customers but may face higher unit costs and limited finance.\n\nLarge-scale production uses greater productive capacity and may achieve economies of scale: reductions in average cost as output increases.\nInternal economies arise within a growing firm, such as bulk purchasing, specialised machinery or management expertise.\nExternal economies arise from advantages available to firms in an industry or location, such as better shared infrastructure or a pool of skilled workers.\n\nGrowth can also create DISECONOMIES OF SCALE, including coordination difficulties, communication delays and bureaucratic waste.\n\nWORKED COST EXAMPLE\nA workshop spends ₦120,000 producing 100 units.\nAverage cost = ₦120,000 ÷ 100 = ₦1,200 per unit.\nAfter better organisation, it spends ₦200,000 producing 200 units.\nAverage cost = ₦200,000 ÷ 200 = ₦1,000 per unit.\nAverage cost falls by ₦200 per unit. This illustrates a possible economy of scale, though lower average cost alone does not identify its precise cause.\n\n9. PRODUCTIVITY AND EFFICIENCY\nProductivity measures output relative to an input, often labour time or number of workers.\n\nLabour productivity = Total output ÷ Labour input.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nA workshop produces 480 school bags with 12 workers over a comparable period.\nOutput per worker = 480 ÷ 12 = 40 bags.\nAfter training and improved tools, 12 workers produce 600 bags.\nNew output per worker = 600 ÷ 12 = 50 bags.\nIncrease = 10 bags per worker, or (10 ÷ 40) × 100 = 25%.\n\nProductivity is not the same as profitability. A factory can produce more units per worker but still lose money if costs rise sharply or customers refuse to buy its products.\n\n10. FACTOR MOBILITY AND LOCATION\nOccupational mobility is the ability of a factor—especially labour—to move from one job or use to another. Geographical mobility is movement between places. Training can improve occupational mobility; housing costs and family responsibilities can limit geographical mobility.\n\nLand cannot physically move from Enugu to Abuja, but land use can change. Capital equipment can often be relocated, although moving a large installed factory may be expensive. Entrepreneurs can transfer their skills and investment across activities.\n\n11. EXAMINATION DISTINCTIONS\nProduction versus manufacturing: production includes goods and services; manufacturing is one branch of secondary production.\nPrimary versus secondary: primary obtains resources from nature; secondary transforms materials or constructs works.\nCapital versus money: physical capital helps produce; money finances acquisition and payments.\nCapital good versus intermediate good: capital goods serve repeatedly; intermediate goods are used up or transformed.\nSpecialisation versus division of labour: specialisation is concentration; division of labour separates stages of one process.\nProductivity versus profit: productivity compares output to inputs; profit compares revenue to costs.\n\n12. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE WITH EXPLANATIONS\nQ1. A firm extracts granite from a quarry. What stage of production is this?\nAnswer: Primary production. It obtains a natural resource.\n\nQ2. A company builds a bridge. What stage of production?\nAnswer: Secondary production, specifically construction.\n\nQ3. Which factor of production receives profit as its conventional reward?\nAnswer: Enterprise. The entrepreneur organises resources and bears risk.\n\nQ4. A business buys a delivery van to distribute products. How is the van classified?\nAnswer: A capital good, because it supports ongoing business operations.\n\nQ5. A bakery uses flour to make bread. Is the flour a capital good or intermediate good?\nAnswer: Intermediate good, because it is transformed into the final product.\n\nQ6. A factory assigns cutting, stitching and inspection to different workers. What arrangement is shown?\nAnswer: Division of labour.\n\nQ7. Why can division of labour reduce unit costs?\nAnswer: Specialisation may improve speed, reduce switching time and permit efficient use of equipment.\n\nQ8. What is a major risk of extreme division of labour?\nAnswer: A breakdown at one stage can interrupt other stages; repetitive work can also cause boredom.\n\nQ9. Twelve workers produce 360 units in a day. What is output per worker?\nAnswer: 360 ÷ 12 = 30 units per worker per day.\n\nQ10. A machine is used for five years in production. Is it normally fixed or circulating capital?\nAnswer: Fixed capital, because it is a durable productive asset.\n\n13. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Tertiary production includes trade and professional services, not only transportation.\n• Secondary production includes construction as well as factory manufacturing.\n• The reward of land is rent; do not confuse it with the purchase price of land.\n• Money is financial capital in business usage but is not itself physical productive machinery.\n• The entrepreneur need not personally own every factor of production.\n• Division of labour is a form of specialisation, not an exact synonym for all specialisation.\n• Higher output does not necessarily mean higher productivity if inputs increased even faster.\n• Higher productivity does not guarantee profit.\n• An item may be a consumer good in one use and a capital good in another.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nPRODUCTION: Primary → Secondary → Tertiary.\nFACTORS: Land → Rent; Labour → Wages; Capital → Interest; Enterprise → Profit.\nDIVISION OF LABOUR: Different people perform different stages of one process."}},{"id":"enterprise-location","old":{"title":"Starting, locating and expanding a business","body":"A business supplies goods or services with stated objectives, commonly including profit, survival, growth and customer satisfaction. Before starting, an entrepreneur should understand the proposed activity, identify customers, estimate costs and obtain suitable finance. Knowledge and feasibility come before assuming that registration alone guarantees success.\n\nLocation is the geographical place where a business operates. A factory may need access to raw materials, power, water, labour and transport. A retail shop often needs convenient access to customers. The weight, perishability and cost of moving goods matter. Cement production usually benefits from proximity to bulky mineral materials; a neighbourhood bakery benefits from closeness to buyers and regular supplies.\n\nOther location factors include land costs, security, government rules, waste disposal, reliable electricity and communication. No factor is always the most important for every enterprise. For an examination question, identify the type of business and the specific constraint mentioned. A firm should also consider competition, the size of the market and the possibility of future expansion.\n\nBusinesses can grow internally by opening branches, increasing capacity or introducing products. External growth involves combining with other firms. A horizontal merger joins businesses at the same stage in the same industry. A vertical merger joins firms at different stages of producing or distributing a related product. A conglomerate merger combines businesses in unrelated activities. A holding company controls another company through ownership of sufficient voting interests.\n\nA joint venture is a business arrangement for a particular project or purpose, often for a limited period. Each participant may contribute finance, technology, distribution or expertise. A consortium is an association of organizations cooperating on an undertaking too large for one alone. Neither term automatically means that all participating businesses cease to exist separately.\n\nWorked example: a cloth manufacturer and a clothing retailer combining are linked vertically. Two competing flour mills combining form a horizontal merger. A flour mill joining an unrelated insurance business is a conglomerate combination.\n\nRetrenchment reduces activities when resources or demand are inadequate. Consolidating operations can concentrate work in fewer locations and reduce duplication. These responses differ from diversifying into additional products or industries.\n\nMerger consideration can include shares, cash or both. A premium may attract acceptance, but neither a premium nor shares are guaranteed in every real transaction.\n\nA cartel coordinates otherwise separate producers, commonly concerning output or prices. OPEC illustrates coordination among oil-producing countries.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Location is not determined by raw materials in every industry.\n• A joint venture concerns a specific undertaking; an ordinary partnership can continue indefinitely.\n• A market opportunity is not enough without finance, relevant knowledge and operating resources.\n• Historical questions may use older labels for forms of business combinations; compare their actual activities."},"new":{"title":"Starting, locating and expanding a business","body":"A business supplies goods or services with stated objectives, commonly including profit, survival, growth and customer satisfaction. Before starting, an entrepreneur should understand the proposed activity, identify customers, estimate costs and obtain suitable finance. Knowledge and feasibility come before assuming that registration alone guarantees success.\n\nLocation is the geographical place where a business operates. A factory may need access to raw materials, power, water, labour and transport. A retail shop often needs convenient access to customers. The weight, perishability and cost of moving goods matter. Cement production usually benefits from proximity to bulky mineral materials; a neighbourhood bakery benefits from closeness to buyers and regular supplies.\n\nOther location factors include land costs, security, government rules, waste disposal, reliable electricity and communication. No factor is always the most important for every enterprise. For an examination question, identify the type of business and the specific constraint mentioned. A firm should also consider competition, the size of the market and the possibility of future expansion.\n\nBusinesses can grow internally by opening branches, increasing capacity or introducing products. External growth involves combining with other firms. A horizontal merger joins businesses at the same stage in the same industry. A vertical merger joins firms at different stages of producing or distributing a related product. A conglomerate merger combines businesses in unrelated activities. A holding company controls another company through ownership of sufficient voting interests.\n\nA joint venture is a business arrangement for a particular project or purpose, often for a limited period. Each participant may contribute finance, technology, distribution or expertise. A consortium is an association of organizations cooperating on an undertaking too large for one alone. Neither term automatically means that all participating businesses cease to exist separately.\n\nWorked example: a cloth manufacturer and a clothing retailer combining are linked vertically. Two competing flour mills combining form a horizontal merger. A flour mill joining an unrelated insurance business is a conglomerate combination.\n\nRetrenchment reduces activities when resources or demand are inadequate. Consolidating operations can concentrate work in fewer locations and reduce duplication. These responses differ from diversifying into additional products or industries.\n\nMerger consideration can include shares, cash or both. A premium may attract acceptance, but neither a premium nor shares are guaranteed in every real transaction.\n\nA cartel coordinates otherwise separate producers, commonly concerning output or prices. OPEC illustrates coordination among oil-producing countries.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Location is not determined by raw materials in every industry.\n• A joint venture concerns a specific undertaking; an ordinary partnership can continue indefinitely.\n• A market opportunity is not enough without finance, relevant knowledge and operating resources.\n• Historical questions may use older labels for forms of business combinations; compare their actual activities.\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nJAMB frequently tests the difference between a sensible location factor and an absolute rule, and between horizontal, vertical and conglomerate combinations.\n\n1. STARTING A BUSINESS\nIdentify demand, test feasibility, calculate expected costs and revenues, secure skills and finance, comply with registration and permits, then plan operations. Registration alone does not establish commercial viability.\n\n2. LOCATION IS INDUSTRY-SPECIFIC\nA heavy cement works may locate near limestone to cut bulky-input transport costs. A neighbourhood pharmacy prioritizes customers, accessibility and licensing. A data-service firm may prioritize electricity, connectivity and skills. Compare input weight, market proximity, infrastructure, labour, security, environmental rules and future expansion. No single factor always wins.\n\n3. BUSINESS GROWTH\nInternal growth expands existing operations. Horizontal integration combines firms at the same production/distribution stage (two competing supermarkets). Vertical integration joins linked stages (flour mill and bakery). Conglomerate integration joins unrelated industries (bakery and insurance company).\n\n4. COOPERATION IS NOT ALWAYS A MERGER\nJoint venture: parties collaborate on a specific venture, often sharing risk and resources. Consortium: organisations cooperate on a large undertaking while typically retaining separate identities. Cartel: independent competitors coordinate competition-sensitive matters such as prices, output or markets. Mere cooperation does not by itself prove a cartel; the anti-competitive coordination matters.\n\n5. HOLDING COMPANY AND RETRENCHMENT\nA holding company controls another through sufficient voting power; it need not own every share. Retrenchment cuts activities or costs, while diversification enters new lines. Consolidation brings operations together; it is not automatically diversification.\n\n6. EXAM SCENARIOS\nTwo competing banks combine → horizontal. A cocoa processor acquires a cocoa farm → vertical. A bank buys a furniture company → conglomerate. Several builders bid together for one airport project → consortium or joint venture, depending on their agreement; not automatically cartel.\n\nJAMB TRAPS\n• 'Near raw materials' is not universally the best location.\n• Vertical = different linked stages; horizontal = same stage.\n• A consortium is not defined merely as any voluntary association.\n• A joint venture does not require permanent merger.\n• A cartel needs evidence of coordination that restricts competition."}},{"id":"sole-trade-partnership","old":{"title":"Sole traders, partnerships and agency relationships","body":"A sole proprietorship is a business owned and controlled by one person. The owner supplies or arranges capital, makes decisions, receives the profit and bears losses. The business is usually easy to establish and decisions can be quick. Its limitations include restricted capital, heavy responsibility on the owner and unlimited liability: personal assets may be used to meet business debts.\n\nA partnership is a relationship between persons carrying on business together with a view to profit. Partners combine resources and skills. An ordinary partnership is generally not a separate incorporated legal person like a limited company. Do not assume that every partner's liability is limited. Liability depends on the type of partnership and applicable law.\n\nAn active partner takes part in management. A sleeping or dormant partner contributes capital and shares profit but does not participate in day-to-day management. Such inactivity does not by itself remove liability. A nominal partner lends a name or reputation to the firm rather than genuinely contributing capital and sharing its profits as an ordinary partner. School texts sometimes use passive and sleeping similarly; read the options carefully when both are offered.\n\nA partnership deed is the written agreement setting out the partners' rights and duties. It can state capital contributions, profit-and-loss sharing, drawings, interest arrangements, management responsibilities, admission of new partners and procedures for retirement or dissolution. It is not a prospectus, which invites investment in a company, or an invoice for goods sold.\n\nAdvantages include more capital than one owner may provide, shared work and complementary skills. Disadvantages include possible disagreements, difficulties transferring an interest and uncertainty of continuity when a partner leaves. A disagreement can be reduced by clear terms and proper accounts, but a deed does not guarantee that partners will never quarrel.\n\nWorked example: Ada contributes money and handles purchases, while Bayo contributes technical knowledge and manages repairs. Their partnership combines complementary strengths. If Chika only provides capital and stays out of management, Chika is a sleeping partner, not automatically a nominal partner.\n\nAdmission of a new partner can increase partnership capital. Bankruptcy can lead to dissolution under the relevant agreement and law; a single ordinary trading loss need not automatically dissolve the business.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Sleeping does not mean having no financial interest.\n• A nominal partner is not defined simply as someone absent from work.\n• The deed records an agreement; it does not turn an ordinary partnership into a limited company."},"new":{"title":"Sole traders, partnerships and agency relationships","body":"A sole proprietorship is a business owned and controlled by one person. The owner supplies or arranges capital, makes decisions, receives the profit and bears losses. The business is usually easy to establish and decisions can be quick. Its limitations include restricted capital, heavy responsibility on the owner and unlimited liability: personal assets may be used to meet business debts.\n\nA partnership is a relationship between persons carrying on business together with a view to profit. Partners combine resources and skills. An ordinary partnership is generally not a separate incorporated legal person like a limited company. Do not assume that every partner's liability is limited. Liability depends on the type of partnership and applicable law.\n\nAn active partner takes part in management. A sleeping or dormant partner contributes capital and shares profit but does not participate in day-to-day management. Such inactivity does not by itself remove liability. A nominal partner lends a name or reputation to the firm rather than genuinely contributing capital and sharing its profits as an ordinary partner. School texts sometimes use passive and sleeping similarly; read the options carefully when both are offered.\n\nA partnership deed is the written agreement setting out the partners' rights and duties. It can state capital contributions, profit-and-loss sharing, drawings, interest arrangements, management responsibilities, admission of new partners and procedures for retirement or dissolution. It is not a prospectus, which invites investment in a company, or an invoice for goods sold.\n\nAdvantages include more capital than one owner may provide, shared work and complementary skills. Disadvantages include possible disagreements, difficulties transferring an interest and uncertainty of continuity when a partner leaves. A disagreement can be reduced by clear terms and proper accounts, but a deed does not guarantee that partners will never quarrel.\n\nWorked example: Ada contributes money and handles purchases, while Bayo contributes technical knowledge and manages repairs. Their partnership combines complementary strengths. If Chika only provides capital and stays out of management, Chika is a sleeping partner, not automatically a nominal partner.\n\nAdmission of a new partner can increase partnership capital. Bankruptcy can lead to dissolution under the relevant agreement and law; a single ordinary trading loss need not automatically dissolve the business.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Sleeping does not mean having no financial interest.\n• A nominal partner is not defined simply as someone absent from work.\n• The deed records an agreement; it does not turn an ordinary partnership into a limited company.\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nExams contrast ownership, management, liability and the legal documents used by small businesses. Keep those four questions separate.\n\n1. SOLE PROPRIETORSHIP\nOne owner controls the business, takes residual profit and normally bears unlimited personal liability. Advantages: easy formation, quick decisions, confidentiality and direct incentive. Disadvantages: limited finance, heavy workload, exposure to loss and continuity risk.\n\n2. PARTNERSHIP BASICS\nPartners carry on a business in common with a view to profit. They may combine skills and capital. In an ordinary general partnership, partners may have extensive personal liability and mutual agency; a partner can bind the firm in the ordinary course of its business, subject to applicable law and circumstances. Limited partnership arrangements differ.\n\n3. TYPES OF PARTNER\nActive partner participates in management. Sleeping/dormant partner contributes but stays out of daily management; this does not automatically erase liability. Nominal partner permits their name to be associated with the firm, typically without the ordinary financial participation of a full partner; liability to third parties can arise from holding out. A minor's position depends on the governing law—never assume a minor can freely contract as a full adult partner.\n\n4. PARTNERSHIP DEED\nThe deed records agreed capital contributions, profit sharing, drawings, duties, dispute handling and admission or retirement. It is an agreement between partners, not a company prospectus or share certificate. Absence of a written deed does not always mean no partnership exists.\n\n5. WORKED EXAMPLE\nAmaka runs the shop daily; Bello invests and does not manage. Amaka is active, Bello is sleeping. If Bello's name appears on the door, that fact alone does not make him nominal: his actual investment and rights still matter.\n\n6. QUICK CHECK\nQ: Who usually makes decisions alone in a sole trade? A: The sole proprietor.\nQ: Does a sleeping partner necessarily have limited liability? A: No.\nQ: Which document states agreed profit-sharing ratios? A: Partnership deed.\n\nJAMB TRAPS\n• One owner ≠ one employee: a sole trader may employ many people.\n• Sleeping ≠ nominal; management inactivity does not define nominal status.\n• A partnership deed does not incorporate the firm.\n• Sharing profits is relevant evidence but does not alone settle every legal question about partnership."}},{"id":"companies-liquidation","old":{"title":"Companies, ownership, shares and liquidation","body":"A company is an incorporated business organization with a legal identity separate from its members. It can own property, enter contracts and continue despite changes in ownership. Limited liability means that members' exposure is generally limited to unpaid amounts on their shares or their agreed guarantee, according to the company type. It does not mean that a company can ignore its debts.\n\nA private company restricts public invitations to subscribe for its securities and has restrictions appropriate to its legal form. A public company can invite public investment subject to the law. In the standard examination distinction, it has no fixed maximum number of shareholders. Registration with the Corporate Affairs Commission is incorporation; admission to a securities exchange is listing. A public company is not automatically listed, and an unlisted company is not necessarily a sole trader.\n\nOrdinary shareholders are owners who usually receive variable dividends and bear the residual business risk. Preference shareholders have stated priority over ordinary shareholders, commonly concerning dividends and repayment of capital. Debenture holders are creditors, not owners merely because they provide long-term funds. Interest is a borrowing cost; dividends are distributions to shareholders.\n\nA memorandum traditionally states basic constitutional matters, including the company's objects, while articles regulate internal management. Historical questions may describe an act outside the objects as ultra vires, meaning beyond its powers. Modern company legislation has changed the effect of some constitutional restrictions. Explain the rule appropriate to the question's date rather than presenting every historical consequence as current law.\n\nLiquidation is winding up a company, realizing its assets and settling claims before any balance is distributed. A liquidator carries out that process. An auditor examines accounts; a broker arranges transactions; neither is the same as a liquidator. Insolvency means inability to meet financial obligations, whereas liquidation is a process and can occur in circumstances other than hopeless insolvency.\n\nWorked example: if assets must be sold, creditors' valid claims normally take priority over members' residual capital. Secured-creditor rights, liquidation expenses and statutory preferential claims affect the exact order. Do not teach that ordinary shareholders are paid before creditors.\n\nA realization account records disposal of assets and settlement items during winding up; liquidation expenses, including the liquidator’s fee, are charged there in standard partnership-style realization accounts.\n\nA company’s board directs overall policy and planning; shareholders exercise their ownership rights through the relevant company procedures.\n\nVoluntary winding up involves members’ or creditors’ appointment procedures; not every liquidator is appointed by directors.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Incorporation and stock-exchange listing are different.\n• PLC identifies a public limited company, not a government-owned corporation.\n• Debenture interest is not an ordinary dividend.\n• A simplified examination priority must not erase secured or statutory claims."},"new":{"title":"Companies, ownership, shares and liquidation","body":"A company is an incorporated business organization with a legal identity separate from its members. It can own property, enter contracts and continue despite changes in ownership. Limited liability means that members' exposure is generally limited to unpaid amounts on their shares or their agreed guarantee, according to the company type. It does not mean that a company can ignore its debts.\n\nA private company restricts public invitations to subscribe for its securities and has restrictions appropriate to its legal form. A public company can invite public investment subject to the law. In the standard examination distinction, it has no fixed maximum number of shareholders. Registration with the Corporate Affairs Commission is incorporation; admission to a securities exchange is listing. A public company is not automatically listed, and an unlisted company is not necessarily a sole trader.\n\nOrdinary shareholders are owners who usually receive variable dividends and bear the residual business risk. Preference shareholders have stated priority over ordinary shareholders, commonly concerning dividends and repayment of capital. Debenture holders are creditors, not owners merely because they provide long-term funds. Interest is a borrowing cost; dividends are distributions to shareholders.\n\nA memorandum traditionally states basic constitutional matters, including the company's objects, while articles regulate internal management. Historical questions may describe an act outside the objects as ultra vires, meaning beyond its powers. Modern company legislation has changed the effect of some constitutional restrictions. Explain the rule appropriate to the question's date rather than presenting every historical consequence as current law.\n\nLiquidation is winding up a company, realizing its assets and settling claims before any balance is distributed. A liquidator carries out that process. An auditor examines accounts; a broker arranges transactions; neither is the same as a liquidator. Insolvency means inability to meet financial obligations, whereas liquidation is a process and can occur in circumstances other than hopeless insolvency.\n\nWorked example: if assets must be sold, creditors' valid claims normally take priority over members' residual capital. Secured-creditor rights, liquidation expenses and statutory preferential claims affect the exact order. Do not teach that ordinary shareholders are paid before creditors.\n\nA realization account records disposal of assets and settlement items during winding up; liquidation expenses, including the liquidator’s fee, are charged there in standard partnership-style realization accounts.\n\nA company’s board directs overall policy and planning; shareholders exercise their ownership rights through the relevant company procedures.\n\nVoluntary winding up involves members’ or creditors’ appointment procedures; not every liquidator is appointed by directors.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Incorporation and stock-exchange listing are different.\n• PLC identifies a public limited company, not a government-owned corporation.\n• Debenture interest is not an ordinary dividend.\n• A simplified examination priority must not erase secured or statutory claims.\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nThis topic combines company identity, capital terminology, shareholders versus creditors and liquidation priorities. Precise vocabulary matters more than memorizing a single list.\n\n1. INCORPORATION AND COMPANY TYPES\nIncorporation creates a legal person distinct from its members. A private company and a public company differ in how they may offer securities and in legal requirements. Being public does not automatically mean stock-exchange listing. The Corporate Affairs Commission handles incorporation; an exchange handles listing under its rules.\n\n2. OWNERS AND CREDITORS\nOrdinary shareholders hold residual ownership interests and usually receive variable dividends when declared. Preference shares may carry dividend or capital priorities, subject to their terms. Debenture holders are creditors entitled to contractual repayment/interest; they do not become shareholders merely by lending.\n\n3. CAPITAL LANGUAGE — DO NOT SUBSTITUTE TERMS\nAuthorised/nominal capital is the traditional maximum share capital stated in the constitution in older textbooks; modern Nigerian company law has changed this framework. Issued capital represents shares actually issued. Unissued capital is the portion not issued. Called-up capital is the amount requested on issued shares; paid-up capital is what shareholders have actually paid. Reserve capital traditionally refers to a portion of uncalled capital set aside for winding up, NOT simply the unissued portion.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nA historical exam says 5 million shares of 50 kobo each are available, and 80% are issued. Traditional nominal value total = ₦2.5 million; issued nominal capital = ₦2 million; unissued nominal portion = ₦500,000. Calling ₦500,000 'reserve capital' without an additional reserve resolution is technically inaccurate.\n\n4. MEMORANDUM, ARTICLES AND ULTRA VIRES\nThe memorandum traditionally sets constitutional fundamentals; articles govern internal rules. Ultra vires historically meant beyond the company's stated powers. Modern legal effects differ from older textbook statements, so use the paper's historical context and avoid treating every older doctrine as current Nigerian law.\n\n5. WINDING UP\nLiquidation realizes assets, settles valid claims and distributes any remainder. A liquidator administers the process; an auditor examines financial statements. Liquidation does not always mean fraud or that a business never had value. Creditors and statutory priorities generally precede ordinary shareholders; secured interests and liquidation expenses affect the order.\n\n6. QUICK CHECK\nQ: Is a debenture holder a shareholder? A: No, a creditor.\nQ: Does a PLC automatically trade on an exchange? A: No.\nQ: Is unissued capital automatically reserve capital? A: No.\nQ: Who administers winding up? A: Liquidator.\n\nJAMB TRAPS\n• Incorporation ≠ listing.\n• Limited liability protects members within its legal scope; it does not cancel company debts.\n• Debenture interest ≠ share dividend.\n• Unissued, uncalled, called-up and paid-up capital are different.\n• Liquidator ≠ auditor; shareholders are not first in line for assets."}}];
